      "content": "That is exactly right. Calculating $15 \\times \\tan(21^\\circ)$ gives us the final length.\n\n**Final Answer:** $\\text{AN} = 5,76\\text{ units}$\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{\\text{Opposite}}{\\text{Adjacent}}$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $\\tan(\\theta)$: The tangent ratio of the given angle.\n  * $\\text{Opposite}$: The side directly across from the angle (in this case, $\\text{AN}$).\n  * $\\text{Adjacent}$: The side next to the angle that is not the hypotenuse (in this case, $\\text{MN}$).\n* **Why we use it:** We use this because we have one side and one angle in a right-angled triangle, and we need to find another side. TOA is a simple way to remember that tangent links the two 'legs' of the triangle.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Always check if your calculator is in 'DEG' (degrees) mode before starting trig problems in an exam! If it's in 'RAD' (radians), your answer will be incorrect."
