    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Okay, so $\\tan(21^\\circ) = \\text{AN} / 15$, so $\\text{AN} = 15 \\cdot \\tan(21^\\circ) = 5,76$. So $\\text{P}\\hat{\\text{M}}\\text{N}$ must be $\\arctan(5,76 / 15)$ which is $21^\\circ$."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The student made a 'slip' by assuming the angle for the whole triangle is the same as the smaller one, forgetting that $\\text{A}$ is only the midpoint of $\\text{PN}$. I need to point out that $\\text{PN}$ is twice the length of $\\text{AN}$. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"2 * 5.758\") -> 11.516] [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"atan(11.516 / 15) * 180 / 3.14159\") -> 37.516]",
      "content": "You found the height $\\text{AN}$ correctly, but remember that $\\text{A}$ is only the midpoint of $\\text{PN}$. \n\nIf $\\text{AN}$ is $5,76$, what is the full height of the building, $\\text{PN}$? Once you have the full height, try calculating the angle $\\text{P}\\hat{\\text{M}}\\text{N}$ for the larger triangle $\\Delta\\text{PMN}$."
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Oh, I see! $\\text{PN} = 2 \\cdot 5,76 = 11,52$. So $\\tan(\\text{P}\\hat{\\text{M}}\\text{N}) = 11,52 / 15$. That gives $37,52^\\circ$."
    },
